syntax:
Exceptions are thrown using the `throw` keyword, followed by an expression.  Exceptions are caught using `try-catch` blocks.  The `catch` block specifies the type of exception it can handle. 

```c++
try {
    // Code that might throw an exception
    if (some_condition) {
        throw std::runtime_error("Something went wrong!");
    }
} catch (const std::runtime_error& error) {
    // Handle the runtime error
    std::cerr << "Error: " << error.what() << std::endl;
} catch (const std::exception& error) {
    // Handle other standard exceptions
    std::cerr << "Standard exception: " << error.what() << std::endl;
} catch (...) {
    // Handle any other exception
    std::cerr << "Unknown exception caught" << std::endl;
}
```
